<article class="env-lemma" id="04PM">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04PM">Lemma <span data-tag="04PM">10.17.9</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. The topology on $X = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$ has the following properties: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$X$ is quasi-compact, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ has a basis for the topology consisting of quasi-compact opens, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the intersection of any two quasi-compact opens is quasi-compact. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The spectrum of a ring is quasi-compact, see Lemma <a href="/tag/00E8" data-tag="00E8">10.17.8</a>. It has a basis for the topology consisting of the standard opens $D(f) = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R_ f)$ (Lemma <a href="/tag/00E4" data-tag="00E4">10.17.6</a>) which are quasi-compact by the first remark. The intersection of two standard opens is quasi-compact as $D(f) \cap D(g) = D(fg)$. Given any two quasi-compact opens $U, V \subset X$ we may write $U = D(f_1) \cup \ldots \cup D(f_ n)$ and $V = D(g_1) \cup \ldots \cup D(g_ m)$. Then $U \cap V = \bigcup D(f_ ig_ j)$ which is quasi-compact. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>